  "query_preview": "..\n\nPseudo-zonal flow maintains benign conditions across the region this \nmorning before a significant pattern shift arrives later today and \ncarries through the late week. This shift is driven by a powerful \nPacific jet streak working into western BC, causing downstream \ntrough amplification from Hudson Bay all the way down to the Mid-\nAtlantic states. A shortwave embedded within NW flow over the \nMidwest first reaches the local area this evening with the attendant \nheight falls and PVA ushering in deepening moisture with time. This \nbrings a 40-60% chance of mainly light rain late afternoon and into \nthis evening. A second shortwave originating from central Canada \nreaches the Great Lakes late this evening, marking the lead edge of \nan arctic air mass. As these two waves merge overhead, a deepening \nlow will emerge east of the area and facilitate a period of \nprolonged northerly cold air advection Thursday into Saturday.\n\nThe initial cold front is marked by an uptick in convective rain \nshower coverage and gusty northeast winds Thursday morning. This \nprecip works through from north to south with a brief lull before \nthe inbound cold air induces enough over-lake instability for lake \neffect showers to kick off. Prolonged NNE sfc-850mb flow maintained \nover Lake Huron allows a multi-band character to emerge Thursday \nevening, reaching peak strength Friday morning as the next spoke of \nPVA arrives within deep cyclonic flow wrapping around the sprawling \nupper low retrograding into the northern Great Lakes. These bands \nwill be predisposed to set up over the western/central Thumb and far \neastern Thumb, including the Port Huron vicinity. Daytime \ntemperatures hold in the 40s on Thursday with rain the predominant p-\ntype, then melting snow gradually takes over Thursday night into \nFriday.\n\n850mb temps fall to around -6C Thursday night, setting up delta-T \nover the lake of nearly 20C. Model soundings highlight 400-700 J/kg \nof lake induced CAPE and impressive boundary layer omega supporting \nlocalized moderate to heavy precip rates where the bands ultimately \nset up through Friday morning. Despite the colder air and heavier \nrates, ambient dew points in the lower 30s and near record high \nwater temperatures (mid to upper 50s F) in the Lake Huron southern \nbasin will result in significant melting of hydrometeors. Immediate \nlakeshore areas stay mild enough to see nil snow accumulation, but \njust a few miles inland WBZ heights falling below 1000 ft will offer \npotential for pockets of wet accumulations on grassy surfaces to \naround 1\" by noon Friday. This is supported by 70-80% probability \nfor minor accumulations in NBM and REFS ensemble data. A small \nhandful of individual ensemble members and operational model runs \nadvertise extreme snowfall amounts but these appear to be driven by \nunrealistic SLRs given the anticipated thermal profile and warm \nground.\n\nSynoptic flow backs to NNW Friday night, Saturday, and likely into \nSunday which confines the lake effect banding to the northeastern \nThumb. Elsewhere, subsidence maintains dry conditions and looks to \neventually reduce coverage of lake effect clouds. The 850mb thermal \ntrough keeps temps on the cool side of normal through the weekend - \nhighs in the 40s and lows in the 20s/lower 30s. A period of \nrebounding heights ensues early next week as the longwave trough \nreleases eastward. This favors dry and stable conditions with a \ngradual warming trend.",
